<BaseLayout
  title="Research | Evan Hobson"
  description="Cattle habitat selection, feral horse movement and rangeland fieldwork."
>
  <section class="page">
    <h1>Research</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      Most of my work uses GPS collar data to ask where grazing animals spend their time and what
      the answer depends on. I work in the BYU Rangeland Ecology Lab with April Hulet.
    </p>

    <div class="list">
      <article class="item" id="cattle-rsf">
        <h2>Cattle resource selection in cheatgrass-invaded rangelands during dormant-season grazing</h2>
        <p class="meta">Lead author · manuscript with coauthors for review · target: Rangeland Ecology &amp; Management</p>
        <p>
          Cows grazed the Spring Hills allotment near Malad, Idaho, in late October and November of
          2021 and 2022 as part of a project meant to suppress cheatgrass. I fit resource selection
          functions to GPS locations from 64 cow-year deployments (31 cows in 2021, 33 in 2022). The
          models are identical except for the cheatgrass layer, which comes from one of three
          remote-sensing products.
        </p>
        <p>
          The species-specific product (USGS REPMS) fit best. With it, cows used areas with more
          cheatgrass less in 2021 and more in 2022. The two RAP products did not show that clear year
          difference. With two years of data I can't say why the sign changed, since year is
          confounded with weather, stocking and vegetation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item" id="horses">
        <h2>Seasonal and monthly variation in feral horse movement and space use in northwestern Nevada</h2>
        <p class="meta">Manuscript in preparation</p>
        <p>
          Legacy GPS data from 11 feral horses, recorded every 30 minutes for between two months and
          two years. I measure daily distance traveled and daily space use (as daily minimum convex
          polygons, used as a movement metric rather than a home range) and compare them with water
          availability and season.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item" id="states">
        <h2>Accelerometer behavior states and habitat selection</h2>
        <p class="meta">On hold</p>
        <p>
          The same collars carried accelerometers, and the source study labeled each cow's behavior
          as grazing, resting or walking. This project asks whether selection results change when
          they are split by behavior. It is waiting on the original labeling records.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item" id="field">
        <h2>Fieldwork</h2>
        <p>
          Summer 2026, Rangeland Ecology Lab. Vegetation monitoring for the
          Sagebrush Steppe Treatment Evaluation Project (SageSTEP) and a targeted sheep-grazing study
          in Idaho: line-point intercept, canopy and basal gap, plant density, biomass, shrub volume
          and soil stability.
        </p>
        <p>
          2024 to 2025, Wildlife Ecology Lab. Pygmy rabbit camera and vegetation
          surveys, mule deer collar retrievals and mortality investigations, and cementum annuli
          aging.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
